DOCS["krasnow-grating-filter"].update({
    "description": "Generate Ben Krasnow-style open-path gratings by treating the selected material's Holographic setting as a calibrated 1-micron anchor and scaling layer speeds for different pitches.",
    "intro": "Krasnow Color Grating keeps the ordinary Rasterizer preparation stages, then replaces the colored regions with open parallel-line patches. Artwork luminance controls patch angle, while artwork hue plus a generated vertical grayscale correction chooses among the available non-black diffraction-grating layers.",
    "sections": [
        ("What remains unchanged", ["The source image is resized against the selected, material-backed palette, quantized to those available colors, and cleaned into closed regions by the normal pipeline. No separate palette is loaded, and other filters and image presets do not use this source-Black behavior."]),
        ("Black geometry", ["Black pixels classified from the quantized source raster are retained as the Black layer's original closed geometry. The preset does not construct the normal synthetic Black canvas, subtract colored regions from it, or duplicate colored paths onto Black for LightBurn punch-through."]),
        ("Open-path geometry", ["Each local non-black patch is filled with parallel LineString paths clipped to the prepared artwork. The defaults match Ben's 0.4 mm patch size and 0.06 mm line spacing. The source luminance maps from -90 to +90 degrees by default, matching his grayscale-angle convention.", "Every available non-black output layer is exported in LightBurn Line mode. The grating paths are open lines; source Black remains closed geometry on its original Black layer."]),
        ("Holographic calibration anchor", ["The selected material must contain an entry whose description or setting name matches Holographic. The filter assumes that recipe's speed and frequency produce a calibrated 1.00 µm pulse pitch on the user's machine. It is loaded once as a template and independently cloned onto every non-Black output layer that survived normal palette and Material Library matching.", "Each clone retains its output layer's original LightBurn ID, color, and name. Power, frequency, pulse width, passes, and other values remain equal to the Holographic anchor; only speed changes. Offset Fill entries use their first concrete sublayer, and every clone is forced to Line/Cut because the filter already generated open vector paths. The temporary template layer is not exported."]),
        ("How correction is applied", ["For saturated artwork, hue is rotated and inversely mapped using Ben Krasnow's 15-to-240 control range. A top-to-bottom grayscale ramp is added and clipped to 0-to-255. That result is quantized across however many selected non-black layers are available, in native LightBurn layer order. Near-neutral artwork receives the correction ramp without a hue offset."]),
        ("Pitch extrapolation", ["Pulse pitch is proportional to speed divided by frequency. With frequency fixed and Speed Spread at its default of 1, each target layer uses: layer speed = Holographic anchor speed × target pitch / 1.00 µm. Available layers are spread in LightBurn order from approximately 0.55 to 1.55 µm; a single non-Black layer remains at the 1.00 µm anchor.", "Speed Spread controls how far layer speeds diverge from the anchor: speed = anchor speed × [1 + Speed Spread × (target pitch / 1.00 µm − 1)]. A value of 0 gives every layer the anchor speed, 1 preserves the normal calculated range, and 2 doubles the variation while the 1.00 µm layer stays fixed. The control accepts 0 through 2 in 0.001 increments.", "For a 300 mm/s Holographic anchor and Speed Spread 1, a full 21-level progression becomes 165, 180, 195, and so on through 465 mm/s at 300 kHz. This follows the dominant fixed-frequency speed progression in Ben Krasnow's published LightBurn example while adapting it to any available layer count."]),
        ("Calibration", ["There is no fixed swatch or layer count. Each available non-black color remains a separate grating-level carrier with a calculated speed and microscopic pitch. Layer colors remain organizational identifiers; the physical result comes from their calculated speed/frequency ratio.", "True Black retains only the geometry and setting associated with Black in the source workflow. The filter does not change Black speed, power, frequency, or operation type."]),
        ("Physical limitations", ["The calculation deliberately assumes that the Holographic recipe is a correct 1.00 µm calibration for the user's specific laser, lens, material, focus, and pulse behavior. It does not prove that assumption or compensate for nonlinear machine response. At the default Speed Spread of 1, the calculated speeds range from 0.55× to 1.55× the anchor; at the maximum value of 2, they range from 0.10× to 2.10×. Verify every calculated speed is within the machine's limits, engrave a pitch test, and observe it from the intended lamp and eye positions before producing final artwork.", "The defaults reproduce Ben's supplied approximately 165-to-90 vertical viewing correction, but viewing geometry is installation-specific. Tune the endpoints and curve against the intended installation."]),
    ],
    "related": ["abstract-filters", "color-layers", "diffraction-gratings", "holographic-calibration"],
})

