            ("How to read a manufacturer chart", ["Look for repetition rate on the horizontal axis and maximum average power or pulse energy on the vertical axis. A rising power region at lower frequency usually indicates pulse-energy-limited operation. The knee where the curve reaches rated average power is the applicable base or power-down cutoff frequency. Above that knee, rated average power may remain available while energy per pulse decreases as pulses occur more often.", "Some manuals provide a table instead of a graph, listing minimum frequency, base frequency, maximum frequency, maximum power, and maximum pulse energy for every pulse duration. Do not interpolate beyond a published operating region unless the manufacturer explicitly permits it."]),
            ("What the software power percentage means below cutoff", ["Requesting 100 percent power below the cutoff does not necessarily produce the nameplate average wattage. It commonly requests the maximum output the source allows at that frequency and pulse duration. For example, if the source is limited to 1 mJ pulses at 10 kHz, its ceiling is approximately 10 W even if the nameplate rating is 30 W.", "Controllers and sources can implement limiting differently. Some automatically derate, some reject invalid combinations, and some software interfaces obscure the effective output. The source manual and measured output behavior take precedence over a generic percentage field."]),
